Embedding is a field declaration with no name — the type name becomes the field name:

```go
type User struct {
    Name string
    Age  int
}
func (u User) Greet() string { return "hi " + u.Name }

type Admin struct {
    User          // embedded — no field name
    Level int
}

a := Admin{User: User{Name: "Root", Age: 40}, Level: 9}
fmt.Println(a.Name)        // "Root" — User's field, promoted
fmt.Println(a.Greet())     // "hi Root" — User's method, promoted
```

Executed: both the field and the method arrive on Admin. That promotion is the whole feature — the inner type's exported fields and methods become reachable on the outer type as if declared there (unless shadowed by an outer declaration of the same name).

What embedding is NOT: inheritance. There's no subtype relationship (Admin is not a User — no assignment, no interface satisfaction through the outer type unless the method is promoted onto it), no overriding (an outer Greet doesn't 'override' — it shadows at the outer level; the inner's Greet still exists and is reachable via a.User.Greet()), no dynamic dispatch (the promoted method is the inner type's method, statically bound).

The interface consequence (the interview follow-up): if User satisfies Greeter and Greet is promoted, then Admin ALSO satisfies Greeter — through promotion. That's how embedding composes interface implementations: embed a FileLogger (has Log) and a MemStore (has Save) into App, and App satisfies both Logger and Store — verified with an AppStore interface embedding both. Interface satisfaction composes through struct embedding; type hierarchies don't exist.
